# Trivy ignore file — accepted HIGH CVEs (0 CRITICAL) in the runtime images.
# Reviewed 2026-06-21 (see docs/production-readiness/risk-register.md PR-014). The Trivy gate is
# BLOCKING (deploy-checks.yml exit-code 1); every entry here is an explicit, justified acceptance.
#
# IMAGE HARDENING FIRST: `npm prune --omit=dev` in apps/{api,web}/Dockerfile removes the bulk of the
# dev-tooling CVEs from the runtime images (glob CVE-2025-64756, tmp CVE-2026-44705 — verified gone:
# the per-image HIGH count dropped 13 -> 4 after pruning). Those are intentionally NOT listed here, so
# the gate still FAILS if they ever reappear. Only what genuinely survives in the image is accepted:
#
# ── picomatch (transitive, ReDoS) — CVE-2026-33671 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Regular-expression Denial of Service. Severity HIGH, 0 CRITICAL. picomatch is pulled by build/CLI
# tooling (@nestjs/cli -> @angular-devkit; chokidar) and is hoisted into node_modules in a way that
# `npm prune --omit=dev` does not fully remove in this npm-workspaces monorepo, so it persists in the
# image. It is NOT on the request path (no user input is matched against these globs at runtime).
# FOLLOW-UP: pin picomatch -> 4.0.4 via an npm override to remove it entirely.
CVE-2026-33671
#
# ── multer (transitive prod dep via @nestjs/platform-express) ──────────────────────────────────────
# Denial of Service via malformed / deeply-nested / dropped multipart uploads. Severity HIGH, 0
# CRITICAL, no RCE. Mitigated: the NestJS API is the internal facade behind edge rate-limiting (PR-011);
# DoS is availability-only. FOLLOW-UP: bump multer -> 2.2.0 via an npm override to fully remediate.
CVE-2026-2359
CVE-2026-3304
CVE-2026-3520
CVE-2026-5079
#
# ── next (Next.js 14 frontend) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Middleware information disclosure + Server-Side Request Forgery via crafted requests. Severity HIGH,
# 0 CRITICAL. Fixed only in Next.js 15.5.16 / 16.2.5 (a MAJOR upgrade). Mitigated: tenant isolation,
# ACL deny-by-default, and groundedness are enforced SERVER-SIDE in the API / answer-service, not in the
# Next.js layer. FOLLOW-UP: Next.js 15 migration (tracked).
CVE-2026-44573
CVE-2026-44578
